<resources>

    <!-- Dialogue enhancer steps.

         Why this override exists: with PixelOS' stock list the app showed
         "Unknown" for the dialogue enhancer on every fresh boot of this device,
         and it was NOT a broken backend. Measured on the running unit with
         `setprop log.tag.DolbyAtmos D`:

             DolbyAtmos-DolbyController: getDialogueEnhancerAmount: enabled=true amount=4
             DolbyAtmos-DolbySettingsFragment: dialogue enhancer value 4 unknown

         DolbySettingsFragment.kt:242-250 only shows a label when the value the
         DAP reports back is present in dolby_dialogue_values; otherwise it
         substitutes R.string.dolby_unknown. Upstream ships 0,2,6,9,12 — a
         ladder from some other vendor's tuning. This device never uses 2 or 9.

         The authority is the panel's own Dolby tuning,
         vendor/etc/dolby/dax-default.xml, which is a blob we ship verbatim.
         Its eight profiles use exactly five distinct dialog-enhancer-amount
         values, and no others:

             profile 0 Dynamic            spk 4   hp 4(off) other 4
             profile 1 Movie              spk 6   hp 4      other 4
             profile 2 Music              spk 4   hp 4(off) other 4(off)
             profile 3 Voice              spk 8   hp 8      other 8
             profile 4 Mobility_default   0 / 0 / 0
             profile 5 Mobility_on_the_go 4 / 4 / 4
             profile 6 Mobility_commute   8 / 8 / 8
             profile 7 Mobility_travel    12 / 12 / 12

         => { 0, 4, 6, 8, 12 }. Listing precisely that set means no profile this
         device can select is ever unlabelled, and the shipped default (4) reads
         as "Low" — the same rung Dolby itself puts it on in the Mobility ladder
         (0 / 4 / 8 / 12).

         Deliberately NOT done: patching packages/apps/DolbyAtmos. The value
         ladder is a property of the panel tuning, not of the app, so it belongs
         in the device overlay; and this tree keeps zero patches outside
         device/ and vendor/.

         Verified while diagnosing this, and worth recording because the owner
         asked whether the app does anything at all: it does. The DAP effect is
         live (dumpsys media.audio_flinger: UUID 9d4921da-…, name "DAP",
         implementor "Dolby Laboratories", one client, 48 kHz float in and out
         on the output chain), and every value the app reads back matches
         dax-default.xml profile 0 / speaker exactly — volume leveler enabled,
         bass enhancer off, speaker virtualizer off, GEQ flat, IEQ off. It is
         reading and writing the real effect, not a stub. -->
    <string-array name="dolby_dialogue_values" translatable="false">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>4</item>
        <item>6</item>
        <item>8</item>
        <item>12</item>
    </string-array>

</resources>
